  </p>

  <h2>Caveats, from reading the code</h2>

  <p>
    Two places where the comments and the code disagree. In both, the code
    is the source of truth.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    The <code>ReelDrive</code> comments call <code>reel.speed</code> "pixels
    per frame". It is not: <code>StandardMode</code> multiplies speed by the
    pitch and by elapsed seconds, so <code>spinSpeed: 30</code> moves 30 cell
    pitches per second (half a pitch per frame at 60 fps) whatever your cell
    size. The drive bounds are in that same unit, per frame. Tune speeds in
    cells, not pixels.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    The second is wording only. <code>ReelMotion</code>'s constructor still
    names its parameters <code>symbolHeight</code> and <code>symbolGapY</code>, <code>Reel.update</code> calls its result <code>deltaY</code>, and the <code>StandardMode</code> and <code>StopSequencer</code> headers describe
    a strip "scrolling downward". The behaviour is axis-generic; the names
    are leftovers from the vertical-only first version.
  </p>

